# Living Specs Best Practices

Spec: LivingSpecs
Purpose:
  Synthesize best practices for living specification systems from
  industry research. Extracted from raw HTML resource (prompt 170-.md),
  the Augment Cosmos / Kiro / Spec Kit / OpenSpec / BMAD / Cursor tool landscape analysis.

Owns: references/sdd/living-specs-best-practices.sdd

Source:
  Article: https://www.augmentcode.com/tools/best-spec-driven-development-tools
  Date: 2026-03-07 (updated 2026-06-18)
  Author: Molisha Shah

KeyFinding:
  "Most SDD tools generate a spec and hand off to a separate agent.
   The harder, more useful problem is making the spec govern the code
   and verifying the result, end to end."

ToolLandscape:
  AugmentCosmos:
    type: Living (auto-updating)
    scope: Organization-wide (Experts)
    best_for: Multi-service, org-scale SDLC
    pros:
      - Living specs prevent drift during implementation
      - Organization Knowledge compounds across sessions
      - Reference Experts cover triage through verification
      - Event-driven triggers from GitHub, Linear, Slack, PagerDuty
    cons:
      - Active iteration on a newer platform
      - Limited independent third-party benchmarks
    pricing: Business ($100/month flat) for up to 50 seats

  AmazonKiro:
    type: Static (EARS notation)
    scope: Single agent + hooks
    best_for: AWS-native greenfield projects
    pros:
      - EARS notation with formal requirements analysis (SMT solvers)
      - Three-document system: requirements.md, design.md, tasks.md
      - Agent Hooks automate repetitive tasks
    cons:
      - Specs are static, do not update as implementation evolves
      - Ecosystem lock-in as dedicated IDE
      - Full spec workflow adds overhead for small changes
      - Credits do not roll over
    pricing: Free (50 credits/mo), Pro $20-200/month

  GitHubSpecKit:
    type: Static (markdown)
    scope: None (agent-agnostic)
    best_for: Open-source, cross-agent standardization
    pros:
      - Free MIT license, no vendor lock-in
      - Agent-agnostic (Copilot, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor, Windsurf)
      - Constitution concept provides persistent project context
      - Version-controlled spec artifacts via Git
    cons:
      - Specs are static
      - No multi-agent orchestration
    pricing: Free (MIT license)

  OpenSpec:
    type: Semi-living (delta markers)
    scope: None (agent-agnostic)
    best_for: Brownfield iterative changes
    pros:
      - Brownfield-first with ADDED/MODIFIED/REMOVED delta markers
      - Given/When/Then scenarios
      - Strict approval gates
      - openspec validate --strict
    cons:
      - Specs do not update during implementation (static)
      - No multi-agent orchestration
      - Manual spec drift management
    pricing: Free (open-source)

  BMADMethod:
    type: Static (docs-as-code)
    scope: 21+ role-based agents
    best_for: Framework-heavy enterprise planning
    pros:
      - 21+ specialized agents for full SDLC
      - File-based handoffs with traceable chain
      - Scale-adaptive intelligence
    cons:
      - Steep learning curve (2+ months)
      - High token costs
      - Static specs with file-based handoffs
    pricing: Free (MIT license)

  CursorWithRules:
    type: Pseudo-specs (rules)
    scope: Single agent
    best_for: Developers already in Cursor
    pros:
      - Plan Mode (read-only planning before implementation)
      - .mdc rule files with YAML frontmatter
      - Glob-based rule scoping
    cons:
      - Pseudo-specs (no automated validation)
      - Rule activation can be inconsistent
      - No living spec capability
    pricing: $20/mo (Pro)

EvaluationCriteria:
  SpecificationLifecycle:
    Living: Augment Cosmos, OpenSpec (semi-living delta markers)
    Static: Kiro, GitHub Spec Kit, BMAD-METHOD, Cursor, Tessl
    rule: Living specs prevent drift during implementation

  OrchestrationScope:
    OrganizationWide: Augment Cosmos
    MultiAgent: BMAD-METHOD (21+ agents)
    SingleAgent: Kiro, Cursor
    None: GitHub Spec Kit, OpenSpec

  OrganizationalMemory:
    Cosmos: Organization Knowledge layer (cross-session learning)
    ECC: Session summaries + instincts + log files
    SDDRA: .sdd/runtime/memory-model.sdd (4-tier memory)

  VerificationArchitecture:
    Cosmos: Agents reference updated contracts automatically
    Kiro: Agent Hooks (tests)
    SDDRA: Quality gates (R1 stage), OBS events

BestPractices:
  1. Specification lifecycle is the FIRST question before adopting any SDD tool.
     Static spec tools produce documents that drift from implementation within hours.

  2. Living specs prevent drift during implementation by auto-updating as code changes.

  3. Verification before and after code generation is critical:
     "The harder, more useful problem is making the spec govern the code
      and verifying the result, end to end."

  4. Organizational memory as a key differentiator:
     Knowledge should carry across sessions, not reset each time.

  5. Context isolation via sub-agents produces better output than asking one
     general agent to plan, code, test, and review in the same context.

  6. Spec detail level must depend on risk level (low criticality = greater acceleration).

  7. Humans review at every phase boundary (spec → plan → tasks → code).

  8. Each spec element should collapse to a single testable claim (EARS notation).

  9. Keep specification and execution plan as separate documents (spec = what, plan = how).

  10. Individual specs should reference shared requirements, not repeat them.

SDDRAAlignment:
  LivingSpecs:
    SDDRA supports living specs via .sdd/evolution/ pipeline
    and temporal knowledge system (.sdd/timeline/)

  OrganizationalMemory:
    SDDRA maps to this via .sdd/runtime/memory-model.sdd
    (4-tier: Long-Term, Project, Task, Working memory)

  OrchestrationScope:
    SDDRA supports organization-wide via chain graph + ECC bridge
    (67 agents → 14 SDDRA roles + 23 specialty roles)

  Verification:
    SDDRA supports verification via R1 review gate + sdd-health command
    and quality gates (.sdd/gates/)

Rules:
  [LIVING-01] Living specs MUST auto-update when implementation changes.
  [LIVING-02] Static specs MUST include explicit drift detection mechanisms.
  [LIVING-03] Spec detail level MUST depend on risk level (risk-adjusted).
  [LIVING-04] Verification MUST occur before AND after code generation.
  [LIVING-05] Spec and execution plan MUST be separate documents.

